{
 "_doc": "MCR-SEC-020 (condition E5) — recorded flag-dictionary coverage, per release and tool. Q19 measures the same thing on every build and FAILS when a tool's shortfall GROWS. It does not fail on the shortfall itself, which is an extractor defect owned by CR-T-09/10, not by the content in this branch.",
 "_method": "Distinct LONG options (--name) found in the committed raw capture under content-src/raw/<rel>/<tool>*.man.txt, against the distinct long option names the dictionary content/flags_<rel>.json carries for that tool. Long options only, deliberately: they are the countable class in man-page text, and the option MCR-SEC-020 was raised about (--add-rich-rule) is one. Short options in prose are too noisy to count honestly, so this gate does not pretend to.",
 "_overcount": "The raw count is an upper bound and includes prose and subcommand options the tool's top-level parser never sees (podman's --foo example, dnf's per-command options). That is why the gate is a REGRESSION gate against these recorded numbers rather than a coverage percentage with a threshold invented here.",
 "_accepted_on": "2026-09-17",
 "_accepted_by": "Milo Vance, Toolkit Systems Engineer — under condition E5 of Marcus Reed's D4 review",
 "_ticket": "CR-T-09/10 (extractor) — the shortfall is theirs to close; MCR-SEC-020 is closed here as ACCEPTED WITH A DATE plus this gate",
 "_retire_owner": "Caleb Stone (extractor, CR-T-09 follow-up)",
 "_retire_by": "2026-09-25",
 "_status": "retired",
 "_retired_on": "2026-09-20",
 "_retired_by": "Jordan Patel and Zee Carter recovery review, authorized by Benny",
 "_retirement_evidence": "The named MCR-SEC-020 parser defect is closed: --add-rich-rule is present in the independently captured RHEL 8, RHEL 9, and RHEL 10 firewall-cmd dictionaries, and Q20 still compares every dictionary with every committed raw capture. Remaining counts are retained as a regression ratchet because the raw-token numerator deliberately includes prose and per-subcommand mentions; they are not an unbounded parser waiver.",
 "_retirement_plan": "Retired 2026-09-20 after the named firewall-cmd synopsis/wrapped-term defect was verified closed across all three captured releases. Keep this file as Q20's non-regression baseline. Any increase still fails; a decrease still requires tightening. Retirement ends the dated waiver, not the coverage measurement.",
 "_the_finding": "Q15 re-runs the extractor and diffs its output against itself, so a systematically skipped option class produces a byte-identical re-parse and a PASS. Nothing measured the dictionary against the raw captures. Now something does. firewall-cmd is the case Marcus named: 205 long options in the RHEL 8 capture, 16 in the dictionary, and --add-rich-rule among the missing — which is why the three firewall generators correctly carry flags:null and the inspector says 'unverified' rather than inventing an explanation.",
